hat is appropriate the fda has never uh  totally bought that  theory  uh but at least for now it tends to look  primarily at the manufacture of the rui  or less at the laboratory  but but it's certainly an area where  they could become involved if they  wanted to  um  this is there appears to be no  consideration for what the test costs  when considering what will be paid and  why is that  well again very good question um  and  the answer that is somewhat bifurcated  so under the gap-filling process  uh that we were talking about in 2013  in fact one of the criteria that  that the contractors could use  were what is the test cost  and  labs submitted some information to  two contractors when doing that  but  but i think you know the cost of a  particular laboratory test often isn't  particularly significant and the key  part cost  that laboratories wanted contractors to  consider was what is the cost of  research and development of a new test  uh and that's and and there was  and and medicare it wasn't clear that  medicare would really take that into  account  uh and basically what medicare  usually looks at is okay well what's the  cost of actually doing the test what are  the reagent costs what are the labor  costs  what are the equipment costs  um  and  that tends to be the way that medicare  looks at these sorts of things so i  wouldn't say under the old process there  was no consideration of that but there's  a there were a lot of  concern that medicare wasn't going to  look at the full range of costs and  also a lot of laboratories were somewhat  hesitant to give cost information to uh  to contractors as well  now going forward under this new process  um  you know i i think you're right it looks  not at what the test cost to do but what  the market price is and i think the  theory behind that is basically congress  and medicare basically saying  well  you know we we should be paying what the  fair market value is of that of that  test  uh and it's very difficult for us to  look at what the cost is but we can what  we do look at is  you know what is this thing going for in  the market generally and that seems like  a an easier benchmark  of and it's a benchmark that  the government is more comfortable with  of saying okay in doing this we can we  can figure out we can look at this and  develop the price for that  um  the next is it's a lab alters and ruo  test even slightly does the test become  an ldt which can be validated and built  for compliant  uh again  this is a  a question that um  there's a lot of debate over and and  a lot of discussion about  under  clia which is the separate set of  regulations that we didn't really talk  about that much  all laboratories have to be  uh  have to have to go through certain steps  uh with regard to a new laboratory test  and under clia  uh what it basically says is if you take  this thing then you  change it or modify it then you have to  go through certain steps to validate it  uh and  some laboratories basically do take the  position that under clear they're  allowed to do that once they do that  it's a laboratory developed test they  can bill for it they've validated it  uh and that does go on  um  the  the  fda  uh hasn't really spoken out one way or  another about it although i think it's  fair to say the fda would not accept  that  probably doesn't accept that approach  its view is  basically  in that situation you're taking  something which has not been uh is not  intended for research for clinical  purposes and making it into something  for clinical purposes but that is a that  is an approach that a lot of  laboratories use and so far the fda  hasn't really taken action and said  anything definitively one way or another  and let's see the last question that i  have is  some molecular diagnostics  have variable limits of detection below  which  a result may not be clinical clinically  actionable  those tests with enhanced sensitivity  exclude themselves  from cms qualifi